  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "- **`EntityId`, `TentityId` and `TaggregateId` lose their parameterless constructors, which made a new random id: `EntityId.New()`, `TentityId.New()` and `TaggregateId.New()` make one now.** A serializer that found such a constructor built the id with it, and one that couldn't set `Value` afterwards, as System.Text.Json can't, read every id back as a new random one, with nothing failing. Replace `new TaggregateId()` with `TaggregateId.New()`. A `New()` that an id type of your own derived from them declares now hides theirs: declare it `new`.\n- **System.Text.Json writes an `EntityId<TPrimitive>`, `EntityId`, `TentityId` or `TaggregateId` as the primitive value it wraps, such as a `Guid` string, and reads it back as the same id, also as the key of a dictionary, with no converter of your own:** each carries the new `EntityIdJsonConverter` as its `[JsonConverter]`. Where it read them at all, it wrote them as an object holding their `Value`. System.Text.Json doesn't inherit the attribute, so an id type of your own derived from them carries it too: `[JsonConverter(typeof(EntityIdJsonConverter))]`. The converter reads an id through its type's constructor that takes the primitive value alone, and an id type without one fails the first time System.Text.Json serializes it. A converter of your own for these types, in your `JsonSerializerOptions`, still takes precedence, and can be deleted.\n- **Their constructors' parameter is named `value`, as the `Value` it sets,** where it was `id`, and `primitiveValue` for `EntityId<TPrimitive>`: Newtonsoft.Json, with no parameterless constructor to use, reads the ids through the constructor, matching its parameter to `Value` by name. A call that names the argument, such as `new TaggregateId(id: guid)`, names it `value` now.\n- **Requires newer minor versions of other Compze packages:** Compze.Internals.SystemCE 0.12.0-internal (was 0.10.0-internal), Compze.Threading 0.14.0-alpha (was 0.13.0-alpha). Upgrading to this version upgrades each package listed here too, so their changelogs apply to this upgrade as well.",
